WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:09.040 align:start position:19%
December 4th, 1872.
The middle of the Atlantic Ocean. A ship

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:14.480 align:start position:19%
is spotted, sailing all by herself. No
crew on deck, no flag of distress, just

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:20.640 align:start position:19%
a ghost ship gliding through the waves
in total silence. Now, some people will

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:25.600 align:start position:19%
tell you a giant sea monster rose from
the deep and dragged 10 souls screaming

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:32.160 align:start position:19%
into the dark. Others whisper about
pirates, a bloody mutiny, even a rip in

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:38.079 align:start position:19%
time. name itself. But the truth, the
truth might be stranger and sadder than

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:43.840 align:start position:19%
any monster. This is the mystery of the
Mary Celeste, and it begins with a ship

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:50.640 align:start position:19%
that everyone agreed was cursed from the
day she was built. Launched in 1861 as

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:55.280 align:start position:19%
the Amazon, her very first captain died
just days after taking command. Then she

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:01.199 align:start position:19%
slammed into another vessel in the
English Channel. Then she ran ground

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:05.680 align:start position:19%
twice. She was salvaged, sold for a
fraction of her worth, patched up, and

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:13.680 align:start position:19%
given a new name, Peter the Mary
Celeste. By 1872, she had a new captain,

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:19.119 align:start position:19%
Benjamin Spooner Briggs, 37, deeply
religious, and a damn fine sailor. For

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:23.840 align:start position:19%
this voyage, Briggs made an unusual
decision. He brought his family, his

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:27.360 align:start position:19%
wife Sarah, and their 2-year-old
daughter Sophia. Their seven-year-old

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:32.560 align:start position:19%
son stayed behind with his grandmother
to go to school. A small mercy, as it

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:38.079 align:start position:19%
turned out. Seven crewmen rounded out
the party. Germans, Danes, Americans, 10

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:44.159 align:start position:19%
souls in total, bound for Genanoa,
Italy. The cargo, 171 barrels of

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:48.960 align:start position:19%
industrial alcohol, highly flammable.
Remember that it matters later. On

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:54.159 align:start position:19%
November 7th, the Mary Celeste cleared
New York Harbor and sailed east into the

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:58.880 align:start position:19%
gray Atlantic. Sarah wrote letters home,
cheerful, full of faith. She mentioned

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:04.399 align:start position:19%
rough weather, but nothing alarming,
just a family at sea. 8 days later, on

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:09.920 align:start position:19%
November 15th, another Brigantine, the
day Graier, left New York on the very

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:14.720 align:start position:19%
same route. Her captain, David Mohouse,
knew Briggs personally. They had shared

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.959 align:start position:19%
dinner in New York just before sailing.
The day Graier was slower. She trailed

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:24.560 align:start position:19%
the Mary Celeste by more than a week
until the morning of December 4th. About

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:30.480 align:start position:19%
400 m east of the Azors, Morehouse's
lookout spotted a ship behaving

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:36.879 align:start position:19%
strangely. She was under partial sail
yawing wildly, lurching through the

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:42.000 align:start position:19%
waves, and there was no one, no one at
the helm. Mohouse raised his own spy

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:46.239 align:start position:19%
glass. His blood rang cold. He knew that
ship. He sent his mate Oliver Dvau

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:50.640 align:start position:19%
across with a boarding party. What they
found would haunt maritime history

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:56.640 align:start position:19%
forever. The Mary Celeste was completely
deserted. 10 people gone, vanished as if

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.879 align:start position:19%
they had simply evaporated. And here's
the part that chills me. Nothing was

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:08.640 align:start position:19%
stolen. The cargo sat intact in the
hold. 1700 barrels of alcohol minus 9.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.640 align:start position:19%
Nine had leaked out during the voyage.
Keep that in your pocket. We'll come

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:17.920 align:start position:19%
back to it. The crew's clothes, their
boots, their pipes, all in place.

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:24.239 align:start position:19%
Sarah's sewing machine, little Sophia's
things, untouched. There was no blood,

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:28.560 align:start position:19%
no signs of a struggle. No violence
anywhere on that ship. But the lifeboat

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:33.599 align:start position:19%
was gone, and the ship's papers were
missing. Every document except the

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:39.680 align:start position:19%
captain's log. The last entry in the log
was dated November 25th, 9 days before

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:46.720 align:start position:19%
the ship was found. It placed the Mary
Celeste near Santa Maria in the Azours,

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:50.319 align:start position:19%
but she was found 400 m east of there.
She had been drifting. The pumps had

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.599 align:start position:19%
been pulled apart, brass fittings
scattered across the deck as if someone

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:59.280 align:start position:19%
had been frantically trying to repair
them. 3 and 1/2 ft of water sloshed in

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:04.640 align:start position:19%
the hold. Not enough to sink her, but
enough to terrify a captain. The forward

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:09.680 align:start position:19%
hatch stood open. One sail was torn. The
compass housing was knocked clean over

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:14.239 align:start position:19%
and a rope trailed behind the stern. Its
end frayed and snapped. The rope that

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:20.239 align:start position:19%
had almost certainly tethered the
lifeboat to the ship. So, let's put it

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:29.040 align:start position:19%
together. 10 people abandoned a seaorthy
ship in the open ocean, climbed into one

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:34.800 align:start position:19%
small boat, and vanished forever. Why?
Theory one, pirates. But pirates don't

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:40.240 align:start position:19%
leave behind 1,700 barrels of alcohol
and every crewman's valuables. Theory

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:46.400 align:start position:19%
two, mutiny. The crew murdered the
Briggs family and fled, but no blood, no

00:04:43.759 --> 00:04:52.080 align:start position:19%
damage, and no motive anyone could ever
find. Theory three, a water spout, a

00:04:49.759 --> 00:04:56.639 align:start position:19%
tornado at sea, scared everyone into the
lifeboat. Dramatic, but the ship was

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:01.040 align:start position:19%
barely damaged. A water spout violent
enough to terrify 10 people off their

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:06.400 align:start position:19%
ship would have torn that ship apart. It
didn't. Theory four, insurance fraud by

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:10.560 align:start position:19%
the owners. The Jibralta salvage court
investigated exactly that and cleared

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:15.680 align:start position:19%
them completely. Now we reach the
theories that actually keep me up at

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:23.520 align:start position:19%
night. Theory five, the alcohol. Nine
barrels had leaked. The hold was filling

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:27.600 align:start position:19%
with invisible, highly explosive fumes.
Briggs realizes the whole ship is a

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:32.400 align:start position:19%
floating bomb. He does the textbook
thing. evacuate to the lifeboat

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:38.240 align:start position:19%
temporarily until the fuse vent. They
tie the lifeboat to the ship with a long

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:45.520 align:start position:19%
rope. Smart seammanship until the rope
snapped. A tiny overloaded boat. 10

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:52.080 align:start position:19%
people, including a 2-year-old, alone on
the Atlantic. The Mary Celeste sailed on

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.680 align:start position:19%
without them. In 2006, scientists
actually tested this. They built a

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:01.360 align:start position:19%
replica hold, filled it with alcohol
vapor, and set it off. The result, a

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:06.080 align:start position:19%
pressure wave whoosh. Terrifying, but
leaving almost no damage, exactly

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:10.800 align:start position:19%
matching the Mary Celeste's condition.
Theory six, the chronometer. Briggs's

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:15.919 align:start position:19%
navigation instruments may have been
faulty, and he believed land was just

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:21.120 align:start position:19%
over the horizon. If you think rescue is
a day away, abandoning ship feels

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:25.120 align:start position:19%
survivable. If you're actually in the
middle of nowhere, it's a death

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:32.000 align:start position:19%
sentence. And then there's the wildest
theory of all. A seaquake. An underwater

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:37.039 align:start position:19%
earthquake shaking the ship so violently
the crew fled in terror. No proof, but

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:42.639 align:start position:19%
the Azor sit on a fault line and that
ocean is restless. I'll admit it's a fun

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:47.520 align:start position:19%
one. As for the sea monster, look, I'd
love for a Kraken to be the answer, but

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:52.639 align:start position:19%
there isn't a single mark on that hull.
Sorry. Now, my personal theory, I think

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:58.000 align:start position:19%
Captain Briggs made the most human
mistake. imaginable. He panicked. What

00:06:55.919 --> 00:07:02.960 align:start position:19%
really gets me about this case is that
disassembled pump. Somebody down there

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:10.720 align:start position:19%
was fighting desperately to save the
ship and losing. Rising water, alcohol

00:07:07.199 --> 00:07:16.479 align:start position:19%
fumes, a torn sail, maybe a storm
building on the horizon. In Briggs's

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:21.039 align:start position:19%
mind, the Mary Celeste was already lost,
so he chose the lifeboat. The wrong

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:26.400 align:start position:19%
call, but an understandable one.
Honestly, in his boots, in that moment,

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:32.000 align:start position:19%
I might have done the same. They tied
off, climbed down, and trusted that rope

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:37.360 align:start position:19%
with 10 lives. The ocean had other
plans. The day Gracia's crew sailed the

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:42.160 align:start position:19%
ghost ship to Gibralar, the salvage
court awarded them a fraction of her

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:47.440 align:start position:19%
value. The judges smelled foul play, but
could prove nothing. Case closed,

00:07:44.319 --> 00:07:52.160 align:start position:19%
mystery opened. 12 years later, a young
doctor named Arthur Conan Doyle wrote a

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.960 align:start position:19%
fictional story about the case and
invented half the facts people still

00:07:54.319 --> 00:08:01.759 align:start position:19%
quote today. Yes, the Sherlock Holmes
guy. He even misspelled her name as the

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:06.800 align:start position:19%
Marie Celeste. The wrong name stuck for
a century. The real Mary Celeste kept

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:10.879 align:start position:19%
sailing under new owners for 13 more
years until her final captain

00:08:09.039 --> 00:08:15.759 align:start position:19%
deliberately wrecked her on a heighten
reef. for the insurance money. The one

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:22.000 align:start position:19%
confirmed fraud in her entire strange
life, and it wasn't even a mystery. No

00:08:18.319 --> 00:08:28.879 align:start position:19%
bodies were ever found. Not one. The
Atlantic kept it secret, and it keeps it

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:34.560 align:start position:19%
still. 150 years on, the Mary Celeste
remains the gold standard of unsolved

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:40.399 align:start position:19%
mysteries at sea. So, what do you think
happened? Panic over fumes, a snapped

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:45.279 align:start position:19%
tow rope, or something stranger? Drop
your theory in the comments. I read

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.279 align:start position:19%
every single one.

